## Context

This is the follow up of [`issue-4127`](https://github.com/redhat-developer/vscode-java/issues/4127) posted in the `vscode-java` repo  :
>  When using the field `sources` in `java.project.referencedLibraries` expressions that should be expanded (i.e. `~` and `${ENV_VAR}`) seem to not work properly. 
> 
>  **Additional context**
> 
> - The expansions work properly for the `include` field. 
> - I have tried to use absolute paths  with no expansion and it works as intended. 
> - As the expansion method seems to be the same for `include` and `sources`, I'm guessing that something goes wrong in how the JSON gets converted to a Java Object.

## Follow up

The issue arises from this code :
```java
public ReferencedLibraries(Set<String> include, Set<String> exclude, Map<String, String> sources) {
        ...
	this.sources = new HashMap<>() {

		@Override
		public String put(String key, String value) {
			return super.put(ResourceUtils.expandPath(key), ResourceUtils.expandPath(value));
		}

	};
	for (Map.Entry<String,String> entry : sources.entrySet()) {
		this.sources.put(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());
	}
}
```
Indeed, depending on the `HashMap` implementation the `putAll` method doesn't always call the `put` method (e.g. https://github.com/openjdk/jdk/blob/master/src/java.base/share/classes/java/util/HashMap.java#L503). This inevitably causes the `sources` paths to not get expended.  
Iterating over the `sources` parameter like so would be a safer way to ensure the paths get expanded properly.

```java
public ReferencedLibraries(Set<String> include, Set<String> exclude, Map<String, String> sources) {
        ...
	this.sources = new HashMap<>() {

		@Override
		public String put(String key, String value) {
			return super.put(ResourceUtils.expandPath(key), ResourceUtils.expandPath(value));
		}

	};
	for (Map.Entry<String,String> entry : sources.entrySet()) {
		this.sources.put(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());
	}
}
```

